data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A production-safe workflow:**\n\n1. **Confirm it’s a leak.** Track `process.memoryUsage()` over time and correlate it with traffic, deploys, and workload. Look for memory that keeps rising across comparable workload cycles instead of settling after garbage collection.\n   ```js\n   const m = process.memoryUsage();\n   console.log({\n     heapUsed: m.heapUsed, heapTotal: m.heapTotal,\n     external: m.external, arrayBuffers: m.arrayBuffers, rss: m.rss\n   });\n   ```\n   `heapUsed` growth points toward retained JavaScript objects; rising `external`/`arrayBuffers` or `rss` without heap growth can indicate buffers or native memory.\n\n2. **Compare heap snapshots.** Capture snapshots at two points under similar load, then compare them in Chrome DevTools. Inspect objects with growing retained size, their dominators, and retaining paths. Snapshots can pause the process and may require substantial extra memory—prefer staging or a canary, and ensure the instance has headroom.\n\n3. **Check common retainers.** Look for unbounded caches or queues, listeners and timers that aren’t removed, closures retaining request data, long-lived session/global references, and buffers or native addons.\n\n4. **Contain risk.** Alert on memory and OOM events, isolate or drain an instance before profiling, and use restarts only as temporary mitigation. Don’t expose the Node inspector publicly; secure any profiling endpoint.\n\nIf heap snapshots are too disruptive, start with low-overhead memory metrics or a sampling heap profiler on a canary."}}

